00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,680
 Alright, having talked about composites and their properties in general for a little while,

2
00:00:03,680 --> 00:00:06,400
 we can now move on to meetings and actual composites.

3
00:00:06,400 --> 00:00:11,200
 In this video we're going to talk about macroscopic composites, those which in reality do present

4
00:00:11,200 --> 00:00:15,040
 as true series or parallel arrangements of their constituents.

5
00:00:15,040 --> 00:00:18,960
 There are a surprising number of real world composites that are actually arranged this way.

6
00:00:18,960 --> 00:00:24,080
 For example, many macroscopic composites are formed specifically to imbue some engineering material

7
00:00:24,080 --> 00:00:28,320
 with some combination of non-mechanical properties that it didn't have before.

8
00:00:28,400 --> 00:00:32,640
 For instance, coding or painting and engineering metal to give it some corrosion resistance

9
00:00:32,640 --> 00:00:34,880
 or to insulate it electrically or thermally.

10
00:00:34,880 --> 00:00:39,760
 But macroscopic composites are also constructed to give an efficient approach to achieving some

11
00:00:39,760 --> 00:00:44,800
 strength or stiffness in terms of their cost or in terms of their weight or in terms of their volume.

12
00:00:44,800 --> 00:00:48,320
 We also very commonly use macroscopic composites, not necessarily by choice,

13
00:00:48,320 --> 00:00:51,200
 but because they're the result of some recycling process.

14
00:00:51,200 --> 00:00:55,040
 Often it's the case that good enough properties can be had from a recycled competitor,

15
00:00:55,040 --> 00:00:59,040
 significant reduction in emitted CO2 and embodied energy in these products.

16
00:00:59,040 --> 00:01:01,520
 In a moment, I'm going to talk about a few specific examples,

17
00:01:01,520 --> 00:01:05,280
 but what you should keep in mind as I do this is that most macroscopic composites

18
00:01:05,280 --> 00:01:08,000
 when loaded in their principal directions, in other words,

19
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:12,320
 kind of along their layers or across their layers, demonstrate exactly the kind of

20
00:01:12,320 --> 00:01:15,840
 series and parallel behavior that we've discussed over the last few videos.

21
00:01:15,840 --> 00:01:19,280
 Think start to get a little bit more complicated if you start to load them out of alignment

22
00:01:19,280 --> 00:01:23,200
 with these principal directions, but we won't get into that so much in this course.

23
00:01:23,200 --> 00:01:26,080
 To kick off, we'll talk first about construction composites because these

24
00:01:26,080 --> 00:01:29,920
 are very, very common in the building industry. Here, they're usually very tight constraints

25
00:01:29,920 --> 00:01:33,600
 on both volume and weight in the materials that you use to make up a building,

26
00:01:33,600 --> 00:01:37,840
 the former obviously because as you increase the volume of say columns that you use to hold

27
00:01:37,840 --> 00:01:41,200
 or floor up, you reduce the floor space that you can then sell.

28
00:01:41,200 --> 00:01:44,080
 And the latter obviously because with increased weight in your structure,

29
00:01:44,080 --> 00:01:48,960
 you require more structure to hold it up and this is kind of a self-perpetuating cycle.

30
00:01:48,960 --> 00:01:52,960
 You couple these strong constraints with the huge economic incentive to minimize

31
00:01:52,960 --> 00:01:57,040
 cost and you've got this perfect breeding ground for the design of composite structures that

32
00:01:57,040 --> 00:02:01,520
 can give you a lot of stiffness and a lot of strength while taking up not very much space

33
00:02:01,520 --> 00:02:04,880
 and not weighing a whole lot and being reasonably cheap.

34
00:02:04,880 --> 00:02:08,800
 We've actually already met one construction composite still reinforced concrete a few weeks

35
00:02:08,800 --> 00:02:11,920
 ago when we were talking about ceramics. And at that point, we talked about that composite

36
00:02:11,920 --> 00:02:16,720
 in the context of pre-stressing concrete to give it some tensile loading capacity.

37
00:02:16,800 --> 00:02:20,480
 Well, it turns out that you can make a composite of steel and concrete and even without

38
00:02:20,480 --> 00:02:24,560
 pre-stressing it, the steel will give the composite some tensile loading capacity.

39
00:02:24,560 --> 00:02:27,760
 Given that concrete is a relatively cheap way to fill a volume, it has a really,

40
00:02:27,760 --> 00:02:29,280
 really good compressive strength.

41
00:02:29,280 --> 00:02:33,760
 Addition of just a little bit of steel to form a concrete steel composite means you can get

42
00:02:33,760 --> 00:02:36,880
 that tensile loading capacity at a relatively low cost.

43
00:02:36,880 --> 00:02:41,200
 Factually, often see steel concrete composites used in a range of civil applications.

44
00:02:41,200 --> 00:02:46,000
 Many construction columns are made, for example, by filling steel tubes or pipes with concrete.

45
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:49,360
 The concrete provides a very cheap way to get a whole lot of compressive strength,

46
00:02:49,360 --> 00:02:53,440
 and the steel in this case acts primarily as a full work for the concrete but also provides

47
00:02:53,440 --> 00:02:55,680
 some tensile loading and bending capacity.

48
00:02:55,680 --> 00:02:58,480
 The concrete on its own would be quite brittle in tension or in bending,

49
00:02:58,480 --> 00:03:02,080
 and the steel being a relatively thin-walled tube would probably otherwise buckle.

50
00:03:02,080 --> 00:03:05,840
 This composite works out to be more cost effective than either of the other options.

51
00:03:05,840 --> 00:03:09,680
 The concrete on its own would probably need a much, much larger cross-section to have enough

52
00:03:09,680 --> 00:03:12,960
 confidence that it's not going to fail in tension or fail in bending.

53
00:03:12,960 --> 00:03:16,320
 And the steel would probably be prohibitively expensive and difficult to manufacture

54
00:03:16,320 --> 00:03:18,160
 if it were a solid cross-section as well.

55
00:03:18,160 --> 00:03:21,760
 Similar steel concrete columns are made in a bunch of different ways by casting concrete

56
00:03:21,760 --> 00:03:25,680
 around an eye beam, for example, or simply reinforcing concrete with lengths of rebar.

57
00:03:25,680 --> 00:03:30,000
 On a related note, another very, very common type of structural or macroscopic composite

58
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:32,240
 are so-called sandwich structures.

59
00:03:32,240 --> 00:03:36,400
 These are composites that the general characterised by having a thick but lightweight core,

60
00:03:36,400 --> 00:03:39,760
 covered by a thin but strong and stiff outer skin.

61
00:03:39,840 --> 00:03:43,120
 A very common sandwich structure, for example, is aluminium honeycomb,

62
00:03:43,120 --> 00:03:48,400
 which has aluminium sheet skin on the outside and a folded formed aluminium honeycomb

63
00:03:48,400 --> 00:03:49,760
 structure on the inside.

64
00:03:49,760 --> 00:03:52,480
 But you also see sandwich structures of dissimilar materials.

65
00:03:52,480 --> 00:03:55,840
 Very often, aluminium is used to skin polymer foam cores, for example,

66
00:03:55,840 --> 00:03:58,160
 because they're very, very cheap to manufacture.

67
00:03:58,160 --> 00:04:01,440
 In any case, sandwich structures are commonly used to sustain bending loads,

68
00:04:01,440 --> 00:04:05,680
 because you can get a very, very large thickness in the direction of bending without adding

69
00:04:05,680 --> 00:04:06,800
 substantial weight.

70
00:04:06,800 --> 00:04:11,520
 And as you've already seen in civil 1100 and will revise more in your ladyy design courses,

71
00:04:11,520 --> 00:04:16,160
 increasing that thickness in the direction of bending drastically reduces the stress that

72
00:04:16,160 --> 00:04:17,360
 you see in your part.

73
00:04:17,360 --> 00:04:21,680
 Sandwich structures are used all over the place from the construction industry all the way up to

74
00:04:21,680 --> 00:04:26,320
 very, very high-level motorsport, but where I think their characteristic is in the aerospace

75
00:04:26,320 --> 00:04:26,880
 industry.

76
00:04:26,880 --> 00:04:30,720
 Sandwich structures and particularly aluminium honeycomb are very, very commonly used in the

77
00:04:30,720 --> 00:04:32,720
 fuselage of many aeroplanes.

78
00:04:32,800 --> 00:04:36,240
 And here, in combination with other materials, they provide a very, very lightweight

79
00:04:36,240 --> 00:04:39,600
 approach to building a really stiff and really strong chassis.

80
00:04:39,600 --> 00:04:43,200
 It's important to note that most sandwich structures are held together only by adhesives

81
00:04:43,200 --> 00:04:46,160
 or by braising if they're metal on metal sandwich structures.

82
00:04:46,160 --> 00:04:50,160
 And consequently, they're at risk of delamination if loaded in tension or if a shear load is

83
00:04:50,160 --> 00:04:51,600
 introduced between the layers.

84
00:04:51,600 --> 00:04:54,960
 This is an aspect that's taken into account by the designers of any structure that's going

85
00:04:54,960 --> 00:04:56,720
 to include a sandwich composite.

86
00:04:56,720 --> 00:05:00,000
 And it's something that you should keep in mind whenever you plan to use one of these as well.

87
00:05:00,080 --> 00:05:03,760
 Finally, on note that sandwich structures aren't only used for their mechanical load bearing

88
00:05:03,760 --> 00:05:07,440
 ability, but are also very commonly used for their non-mechanical properties as well.

89
00:05:07,440 --> 00:05:10,880
 Sandwich structures that include polymer foams, for example, are very commonly used in the

90
00:05:10,880 --> 00:05:15,120
 building and construction industry because they provide a thermal insulation and a acoustic

91
00:05:15,120 --> 00:05:19,840
 insulation while still maintaining a moderate level of strength and stiffness and wear resistance.

92
00:05:19,840 --> 00:05:24,800
 Aluminium composite panels ACPs, for example, are very commonly used in the facades of many

93
00:05:24,800 --> 00:05:25,600
 buildings.

